counterexample:
-- Insert a bar
INSERT INTO Bar (name, address) VALUES ('ThePub', '123 Main St');

-- Insert two drinkers
INSERT INTO Drinker (name, address) VALUES ('Alice', '1 Alice Ave'), ('Bob', '2 Bob Blvd');

-- Insert frequents records. (For the purpose of this counterexample we assume that Frequents has an extra attribute (say, day) so that a drinker can have multiple measurements per bar without violating the primary key. That is, we assume the real data is not perfectly normalized.)
-- For example, imagine the primary key is actually (drinker, bar, day) rather than (drinker, bar).

-- Alice appears on two different days at ThePub, each with 3 times_a_week
INSERT INTO Frequents (drinker, bar, times_a_week) VALUES ('Alice', 'ThePub', 3);
INSERT INTO Frequents (drinker, bar, times_a_week) VALUES ('Alice', 'ThePub', 3);

-- Bob appears once with 4 times_a_week
INSERT INTO Frequents (drinker, bar, times_a_week) VALUES ('Bob', 'ThePub', 4);

-- Insert a beer and a serves record so that AVG(s.price) is defined
INSERT INTO Beer (name, brewer) VALUES ('Beer1', 'Brewer1');
INSERT INTO Serves (bar, beer, price) VALUES ('ThePub', 'Beer1', 5.00);

/*
Expected behavior:
• Q1 computes COUNT(DISTINCT drinker)=2 and AVG(price)=5.00. For the most_reg_drinker, Q1’s subquery runs over each raw Frequents record per bar. It finds two records for Alice (each with 3) and one for Bob (4). Since 4 > 3, it returns 'Bob'.
• Q2 also computes COUNT(DISTINCT drinker)=2 and AVG(price)=5.00. However, its subquery groups the frequent records by drinker, so it computes SUM(times_a_week)=6 for Alice (3+3) and 4 for Bob. Then ordering by the sum descending, it returns 'Alice'.
Thus, the two queries return different most_reg_drinker values for bar 'ThePub'.
*/

